# Case Analysis: Chinachem Charitable Foundation Ltd v. Chan Chun Chuen and Another [2011] HKCA 13

## 1. Facts of the Case

This case concerns a probate dispute over the estate of the late Nina Wang, the former chairwoman of the Chinachem Group, whose fortune was estimated at approximately HK$100 billion. Nina Wang's husband, Teddy Wang, had been kidnapped in 1990 and was presumed dead, though Nina Wang maintained for years that he was still alive.

Nina Wang executed a will on 28 July 2002 ("the 2002 will"), which bequeathed her entire estate to the plaintiff, the Chinachem Charitable Foundation Ltd, a charitable foundation established by Teddy Wang and Nina Wang in 1988. The 2002 will provided that the foundation would be supervised by a managing body comprising the UN Secretary General, the Premier of the People's Republic of China, and the Chief Executive of Hong Kong. It also expressed wishes for Chinese prizes of worldwide significance and for provisions to be made for Teddy Wang's family and the group's staff.

The first defendant, Chan Chun Chuen, was a fung shui master who had been introduced to Nina Wang in March 1992. He claimed that he and Nina Wang had been lovers for many years and that she had treated him as her husband. According to the first defendant, Nina Wang executed a second will on 16 October 2006 ("the 2006 will"), which named him as the sole beneficiary of her entire estate. He claimed that Nina Wang had given him the 2006 will in an envelope on the evening it was signed and told him to keep it secret. He allegedly showed it to his wife two days later and then kept it hidden until Nina Wang's death on 3 April 2007.

The 2006 will purported to be signed by Nina Wang and witnessed by a solicitor, Mr Winfield Wong, and by Mr Ng Shung Mo. However, both attesting witnesses testified that the document they witnessed on 16 October 2006 was very different from the 2006 will—it was a partial will giving a Mr Chan a bequest of approximately HK$10 million, not the document that disposed of Nina Wang's entire estate to the first defendant.

The plaintiff sought to have the 2002 will pronounced in solemn form and challenged the validity of the 2006 will, alleging it was a forgery. The first defendant sought the opposite—that the 2006 will be pronounced in solemn form and the 2002 will be revoked.

## 2. Arguments of the Parties

### The Plaintiff's (Respondent's) Arguments

The plaintiff argued that the 2006 will was a forgery. The key evidence came from the two attesting witnesses, Mr Winfield Wong and Mr Ng Shung Mo, who both testified that the document they witnessed on 16 October 2006 was a partial will dealing with a specific bequest of approximately HK$10 million to a Mr Chan, and not the 2006 will that purported to dispose of Nina Wang's entire estate to the first defendant. Mr Wong specifically recalled telling Nina Wang that it was only a partial will, and she acknowledged this.

The plaintiff further relied on expert handwriting evidence from Mr Radley, who concluded that the Nina Wang signature on the 2006 will was a highly skilled simulation and that the Winfield Wong signature was not genuine. The plaintiff also pointed to the scientific evidence showing that the Nina Wang signature was written over a crease in the paper—a crease that would not have been present if Nina Wang had signed the document in the presence of the witnesses as claimed.

The plaintiff argued that Nina Wang had never deviated from her intention to leave her estate to the foundation, and that the first defendant had fabricated the 2006 will in a quest to secure her enormous fortune.

### The First Defendant's (Appellant's) Arguments

The first defendant argued that the judge had failed to properly evaluate the evidence by reference to inherent probabilities and improbabilities before making factual findings. He relied on statements from the Court of Final Appeal in HKSAR v Egan and Nina Kung v Wang Din Shin to support this proposition.

The first defendant further argued that the evidence at trial did not justify the conclusion that the 2006 will was a forgery. He contended that the judge had not recognized that Nina Wang loved him deeply and that the judgment was tainted because the judge was morally offended by the relationship between Nina Wang and the first defendant. He argued that given Nina Wang's other substantial payments to him (including three payments of HK$688 million each), the suggestion that the document Mr Wong signed related to a gift of only HK$10 million was absurd and derisory—it neither reflected Nina Wang's affection for him nor was it understandable in light of the further payment of HK$688 million made just days after 16 October 2006.

The first defendant also challenged the judge's adoption of Mr Radley's handwriting analysis approach, arguing that Mr Radley had made fundamental errors of principle rendering his conclusions "irrational and unscientific." Specifically, he criticized Mr Radley for: (1) equating "rarities" with "differences"; (2) excluding known samples extracted from Nina Wang's hospital records; and (3) his "no-lose" argument. He also challenged the judge's findings on specific features of both the Nina Wang and Winfield Wong signatures.

## 3. Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

### The Approach to Fact-Finding and Inherent Probabilities

The Court of Appeal affirmed that a trial judge must evaluate evidence by reference to inherent probabilities and improbabilities before making factual findings. However, the court held that the judge in this case clearly did take into account the inherent improbabilities stressed on behalf of the first defendant. The court noted that the judge referred to them at various parts of his judgment (paragraphs 91, 212, 223-228, 275, 300, 371 and 876) and gave more than ample coverage to them in paragraphs 841 to 876. The fact that the detailed analysis came towards the end of the judgment was merely a matter of presentation.

**Key Principle:** Once it is established that the judge did take the inherent improbabilities into account, there is no error that an appellate court can correct. The weight given to each factor is a matter essentially for the judge to decide in making findings of fact. So long as the judge did take them into account, it is not for the appellate court to interfere and say that he should have given more or less weight to any specific point.

### The Standard for Appellate Interference with Factual Findings

The court emphasized that it will not interfere with a trial judge's findings of fact unless there is a clear error. The judge's findings on the credibility of witnesses and the weight of evidence are matters for the trial judge, who has the advantage of seeing and hearing the witnesses.

### The Role of Expert Handwriting Evidence

The court addressed several important principles regarding expert handwriting evidence:

**a) The Definitions of "Similarities" and "Differences"**

The court accepted Mr Radley's explanation that the basis of handwriting comparison is to take known authentic writings, inter-compare them to establish the range of variation for each feature, and then compare the questioned writing to see if corresponding features fall within or outside that range. A "similarity" exists when a feature falls within the range of variation; a "difference" exists when it falls outside. The presence of fundamental differences may indicate different authorship, while an accumulation of significant similarities (in the absence of significant differences) may indicate common authorship.

**b) The Concept of "Rarities" and "Accidentals"**

The court accepted that a "rarity" is a feature that occurs not through accident but during the normal writing process that is rarely found in specimen signatures of known authenticity. An "accidental" is a very unusual occurrence of a feature that may result from external influence. The court accepted Mr Radley's view that "accidentals" and "rarities" tend to merge and that one cannot necessarily differentiate between them—accidentals form a subset of rarities.

**c) The Significance of Similarities and Differences**

The court held that the presence of similarities per se is not sufficient to lead to a conclusion of identity where there are significant differences that remain unexplained. This is consistent with the guidance in Nina Kung v Wang Din Shin, where Chan PJ stated that "the absence of fundamental differences (as opposed to trivial differences) together with the presence of a combination of a sufficient number of similarities with individual qualities and characteristics can form the basis of a conclusion that the signature is genuine."

**d) The Use of Comparison Signatures**

The court addressed the issue of how many comparison signatures are appropriate. While different authorities support different numbers (ranging from a dozen to 40), the court accepted that the size of the sample can affect the rarity value of features. The court held that it was open to the judge to accept Mr Radley's exclusion of 42 hospital signatures because they showed poor pen control and were therefore inappropriate for comparison with a well-controlled questioned signature. The court emphasized that comparing like with like is a cardinal principle in handwriting examination.

**e) The "No-Lose" Argument**

The court accepted the validity of Mr Radley's "no-lose" argument—that when the range of variation in genuine signatures is so great that virtually any variation would fall within it, a match is not significant because it is inevitable and therefore neutral for identification purposes. The court held that the logic of this proposition is self-evident and accords with common sense.

**f) Equating "Rarities" with "Differences"**

The court accepted that Mr Radley's use of the word "differences" carried a special meaning—it referred to features that were different from the vast majority of the large sample of genuine signatures, rather than the accepted definition of that term. The court held that provided the meaning is clear, it is difficult to see why the use of a modified meaning is objectionable per se. The real question is the validity of what the judge referred to as the "Radley-Osborn statistical point."

**g) The Statistical Point and Accumulation of Rarities**

The court accepted the validity of the statistical point—that the accumulation of rarities in a questioned signature is highly improbable and constitutes strong evidence of forgery. The court cited Osborn's Questioned Documents (2nd ed., 1929) at pp. 231-232: "Errors of commission consist in putting in what is not usual and habitual and... those individual characteristics may be so rare that the combination of all of them in one signature is so improbable as to amount to very strong evidence of forgery."

The court also referred to Huber & Headrick, Handwriting Identification: Facts and Fundamentals, pp. 64-66, which discusses Osborn's suggestion of a statistical basis using the Newcomb rule of probability, while noting that Osborn overlooked the need to qualify events as independent events. The court accepted that statistical inference has a vital role to play in handwriting identification.

**h) The Propriety of Expert Evidence and Ambush**

The court strongly criticized the first defendant's attempt to introduce new rarities by reference to specimen 66 during cross-examination of Mr Radley without any prior warning. The judge observed that this was "wholly against the spirit and the terms of the expert directions" and was "an ambush which has no place in modern litigation." The Court of Appeal affirmed this criticism.

### The Weight of Expert Evidence

The court held that the weight to be attributed to expert evidence and its relevance are essentially matters for the judge. The judge is entitled to prefer one expert over another, particularly where the expert's evidence is tested by cross-examination and the judge has the advantage of seeing and hearing the witnesses.

### The Significance of Attesting Witness Evidence

The court held that the evidence of the attesting witnesses was the most important finding of fact. Where both attesting witnesses testified that the document they witnessed was fundamentally different from the document produced, and the judge accepted their evidence after careful scrutiny, that finding alone would debar the first defendant from succeeding. The court emphasized that the judge's finding that the 2006 will was a forgery was "unassailable" and was made in full appreciation of the fact that it carried with it the inevitable conclusion that the first defendant had put before the court a forged document in his quest to secure Nina Wang's enormous fortune.

### Costs in Probate Actions

The court affirmed the principles regarding costs in probate actions:

**a) Indemnity Basis Costs**

The court held that it would be scarcely imaginable that a court could do otherwise than order costs on an indemnity basis given the findings of the judge. Where it was proved that the first defendant knowingly put forward a forged will in the hope of securing for himself an immense fortune, and in doing so told lies, that must on any footing be an egregious abuse of the process of and an affront to the court. Any other order than costs on an indemnity basis would be inconceivable.

**b) Costs of Administrators Pendente Lite**

The court affirmed that it is well settled that the liability for costs of a probate action in general covers the charges of an administrator pending determination of the action and the costs of the application for his appointment. The fact that an appeal was pending was nothing to the point.

**c) Costs of the Secretary for Justice**

The court held that it was reasonable and proper for the plaintiff to join the Secretary for Justice as a party to the action. The costs of the Secretary for Justice were kept to a minimum and were occasioned because of the first defendant's stance in relation to the 2006 will. It was right that the judge should order that the costs of the Secretary for Justice should be borne by the estate and the first defendant should indemnify the estate in that respect.

### The Approach to Further Evidence on Appeal

The court addressed the first defendant's application to adduce further evidence consisting of fee notes presented by Mr Sujanani. The court noted that the fact that 5 hours was charged in respect of the conference does not necessarily reflect that Mr Wong was with Mr Sujanani for the whole of that period, particularly as Mr Sujanani clearly did drafting work after the conference. Nevertheless, since the court was appraised of the matter and since the entry in respect of the conference with Mr Midgley had also been referred to, the admission of the first page of the fee note was acceded to.

### The Principle of Finality and Abuse of Process

The court concluded by stating that the first defendant had persisted in pursuing a thoroughly dishonest case and had abused the process of the court. The court indicated that in the absence of any argument as to costs, it would make an order that costs should be on an indemnity basis with a certificate for 3 counsel.

## 4. Striking, Unusual, or Remarkable Aspects of the Case

### The Sheer Scale of the Estate

The estate was estimated at approximately HK$100 billion, making this one of the largest probate disputes in Hong Kong's history. The sums involved—including three payments of HK$688 million each to the first defendant, investments of £5 million, £15 million, and £30 million in RCG Holdings Limited—are staggering by any measure.

### The Clandestine Relationship

The relationship between Nina Wang and the first defendant was extraordinary in several respects. The first defendant was a married man with three children, and he maintained that Nina Wang understood he would not marry her and that he needed to be with his family. He spent birthdays, summer, and Christmas with his family while conducting a secret relationship with Nina Wang. The judge described this as "a man having a family in which she would not be a member." The relationship was kept secret right up until Nina Wang's death, with the first defendant destroying photos and videos during the probate litigation involving Teddy Wang's will.

### The Fung Shui Master's Role

The first defendant's role as a fung shui master was central to the case. He had been introduced to Nina Wang in 1992 as a fung shui master to help locate her kidnapped husband. Hole-digging exercises were conducted at various sites, including Ap Lei Chau where Teddy Wang's car had been found. The fung shui activities continued even after Nina Wang was diagnosed with terminal cancer, with the first defendant supervising the digging of holes at Chinachem sites. The correlation between Nina Wang's deteriorating health, the increased fung shui activities, and the massive payments to the first defendant is striking.

### The Forgery of a Will

The finding that the 2006 will was a forgery is remarkable in itself, but the manner in which it was accomplished is even more extraordinary. The forged will purported to bear the signatures of Nina Wang and two attesting witnesses, Mr Winfield Wong and Mr Ng Shung Mo. The scientific evidence revealed that the Nina Wang signature was written over a crease in the paper—a crease that would not have been present if the document had been signed in the presence of the witnesses as claimed. The unsigned copy of the will had the impression of the three signatures in exact register with those in the 2006 will, suggesting that the forged document was created by tracing or copying signatures from another document.

### The Attesting Witnesses' Evidence

The evidence of the two attesting witnesses was crucial. Both testified that the document they witnessed on 16 October 2006 was a partial will dealing with a specific bequest of approximately HK$10 million to a Mr Chan, not the document that disposed of Nina Wang's entire estate to the first defendant. Mr Wong specifically recalled telling Nina Wang that it was only a partial will, and she acknowledged this. The fact that both witnesses independently recalled this detail, and that Mr Ng remembered Mr Wong saying at the time that it was a partial will, was powerful evidence that the 2006 will was not the document they witnessed.

### The Expert Handwriting Evidence

The expert handwriting evidence was highly technical and involved detailed analysis of numerous features of the signatures. The court's acceptance of Mr Radley's "no-lose" argument and the statistical point regarding the accumulation of rarities is legally significant. The court's rejection of Mr Westwood's approach—which relied on deteriorated hospital signatures for comparison purposes—is also noteworthy. The court's finding that the Nina Wang signature was a "highly skilled simulation" and that the Winfield Wong signature was not genuine demonstrates the sophistication of the forgery.

### The Judge's Moral Disapproval

The first defendant argued that the judge was morally offended by the relationship between Nina Wang and the first defendant and that this tainted his judgment. The Court of Appeal acknowledged that it would be surprising if the judge did not consider the relationship morally unacceptable, but held that there was no ground for saying that this coloured his judgment. This raises an interesting question about the extent to which a judge's personal moral views can or should influence fact-finding in a case involving an unconventional relationship.

### The Abuse of Process

The court's conclusion that the first defendant had "persisted in pursuing a thoroughly dishonest case" and had "abused the process of the court" is striking. The court's indication that costs would be on an indemnity basis with a certificate for 3 counsel reflects the seriousness with which the court viewed the first defendant's conduct. The fact that the first defendant was found to have forged a will in a quest to secure Nina Wang's enormous fortune is a remarkable finding, particularly given the resources and sophistication involved.

### The Respondent's Notice and the Fung Shui Will

The court briefly addressed the respondent's notice, noting that on the evidence as to Nina Wang's consistent use of fung shui, there must be some possibility that whatever document Mr Wong and Mr Ng witnessed on 16 October 2006 may well have been a fung shui will. Without seeing the document, it would be impossible for any witness, still less the court, to make any definite finding on the matter. This is an intriguing aside—the possibility that the document witnessed was a fung shui will adds another layer of complexity to an already extraordinary case.

### The Size of the Comparison Signature Sample

The court noted that for the Nina Wang signature, 135 specimens were presented to the experts, with Mr Radley accepting 81 as appropriate comparison signatures and Mr Westwood using all 135. The difference of 54 specimens included 42 "hospital signatures" extracted from hospital records. The sheer volume of comparison signatures—far exceeding the typical 15 to 20—reflects the extraordinary resources deployed in this case and the complexity of the handwriting analysis.

### The Fee Notes Evidence

The first defendant's application to adduce further evidence consisting of Mr Sujanani's fee notes is a remarkable detail. The fact that the fee notes were sought to be admitted on appeal to challenge the judge's findings on Mr Wong's evidence demonstrates the lengths to which the first defendant was willing to go. The court's admission of the first page of the fee note, while noting that the 5 hours charged did not necessarily reflect that Mr Wong was with Mr Sujanani for the whole period, is a pragmatic approach to an unusual evidentiary issue.

### The Overall Significance

This case is remarkable not only for its sheer scale and the extraordinary facts involved—a clandestine relationship between a billionaire and a fung shui master, allegations of forgery, and a quest for one of the largest fortunes in Asia—but also for the legal principles it establishes regarding expert handwriting evidence, the evaluation of inherent probabilities, and the consequences of advancing a forged will in probate proceedings. The case serves as a cautionary tale about the risks of forgery and the severe consequences that follow when a party attempts to abuse the process of the court. [end of text]


